sec.p:
Expanding upon Pu and Thomas’ methodology to represent our system, we added one dimension to track the calcium concentration, six dimensions for each possible state of the LVA channel, and two dimensions for each possible state of the BK channel. The state vectors for each ion channel are therefore defined as follows: a five-component vector NK for the K+ gates, an eight-component vector NNa for the Na+ gates, a six-component vector NLVA for the LVA gates, and a two-component vector NBK for the BK gates. The state vectors are represented as:
(1)
NK=[nK0,nK1,nK2,nK3,nK4]T∈[0,1]5 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} N^{K}=\left[n_{K 0}, n_{K 1}, n_{K 2}, n_{K 3}, n_{K 4}\right]^{T} \in[0,1]^{5} \tag{1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

For a sufficiently large number of channels, Schmidt and Thomas (2014) and Schmidt et al. (2018) demonstrated that the equations governing the system can be approximated by a multidimensional Langevin equation, as follows:

(5)

dNv=∑k=1kmaxζv{αkv(V)Ni(k)vdt+ϵvαkvNi(k)vdWkv}
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} d N^{v}=\sum_{k=1}^{k_{\max }} \zeta^{v}\left\{\alpha_{k}^{v}(V) N_{i(k)}^{v} d t+\sqrt{\epsilon^{v} \alpha_{k}^{v} N_{i(k)}^{v} d W_{k}^{v}}\right\} \tag{5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



Where the set v contains the four voltage-gated ionic currents included in the model:

(6)

v∈{LVA,Na,K,BK}
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} v \in\{L V A, N a, K, B K\} \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



Here ζv is the stoichiometry vector for the kth directed edge. If we write i(k) for the source node and j(k) for the destination node of edge k, then ζv=ej(k)v−ei(k)v\begin{document}$\zeta^{v}=e_{j(k)}^{v}-e_{i(k)}^{v}$\end{document}. The voltage-dependent per capita transition rate along the kth edge is αkv(V)\begin{document}$\alpha_{k}^{v}(V)$\end{document} and Ni(k)v\begin{document}$N_{i(k)}^{v}$\end{document} is the fractional occupancy of the source node for the kth transition. The finite-time increment in the Poisson process is approximated by a gaussian process, namely, the increment dWkv\begin{document}$d W_{k}^{v}$\end{document} in a Wiener (Brownian motion) process associated with each directed edge. These independent noise terms are scaled by ϵv=1NTotv\begin{document}$\epsilon^{v}=\frac{1}{N_{T o t}^{v}}$\end{document}, where NTotv\begin{document}$N_{T o t}^{v}$\end{document} represents the total number of that specific ion channel.

The membrane potential is governed by the current balance equation:

(7)

CdVdt=∑χIχ
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} C \frac{d V}{d t}=\sum_{\chi} I_{\chi} \tag{7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



where the set χ contains the six ionic currents included in the model:

(8)

χ∈{LVA,Na,K,BK,L,ORCO}
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \chi \in\{L V A, N a, K, B K, L, O R C O\} \tag{8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



Each of the six ionic currents included in the model are described as:

(9)

INa=−gNaN8Na(V−VNa)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} I_{N a}=-g_{N a} N_{8}^{N a}\left(V-V_{N a}\right) \tag{9} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Each gate’s steady state activation function, except for the BK channel, takes the form:
(16)
pj∞v(V)=1.01.0+exp⁡((V−θp)βp) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} p_{j \infty}^{v}(V)=\frac{1.0}{1.0+\exp \left(\frac{\left(V-\theta_{p}\right)}{\beta_{p}}\right)} \tag{16} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The IBK current is dependent on the intracellular calcium concentration and membrane potential as represented by the following equations:
(17)
m∞BK(V)=1.01.0+exp⁡((V−V12)(−15.6)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} m_{\infty}^{B K}(V)=\frac{1.0}{1.0+\exp \left(\frac{\left(V-V_{\frac{1}{2}}\right)}{(-15.6)}\right)} \tag{17} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
